{"genre": "Reuters", "passage_A": "Since 1949, Taiwan has been de facto independent, posing the biggest geopolitical challenge to China. The self-governing island chain has struck trade agreements with just six countries—China, Japan, the United States, South Korea, Australia, and New Zealand—and its populace holds admiration for the democratic and free-market systems of the European Union.

