{"genre": "Essay", "passage_A": "In Henrik Ibsen's 1879 play 'A Doll's House,' he exemplified many of the hallmarks of the naturalist drama movement that flourished in the late 19th century. Naturalism in drama sought to portray 'real life' on stage as faithfully and objectively as possible. Ibsen embraced this approach in 'A Doll's House' by centering his narrative on the everyday lives of middle-class individuals and their often mundane struggles and interpersonal dynamics.

The play is notable for its deep dive into the psychological and emotional states of its characters, relegating melodrama to the background. Instead, it delves into familial relationships, personal bonds, gender roles, and individual identity. This emphasis on internal, psychological aspects was a defining characteristic of naturalistic drama.

Additionally, Ibsen included extensive stage directions within the script to guide the most realistic and vivid staging of the play. These detailed instructions span several pages at the start of the play, meticulously outlining the set design, furniture, lighting, and daily routines of the characters.", "passage_B": "Henrik Ibsen’s play, A Doll’s House, embodies many of the characteristics of the naturalistic drama movement of the late nineteenth century. The naturalistic drama aimed at presenting “real life” on the stage as truthfully and objectively as possible. Ibsen’s play, A Doll’s House, adopts this method by concentrating on the lives of the middle class and their daily struggles and relationships. The play focuses on the psychology and inner experiences of the characters rather than on melodramatic plots and action. The characters are ordinary people who struggle with family relationships, gender roles, and self-identity. This focus on the inner, psychological aspects of the characters was a hallmark of the naturalistic drama. Ibsen also included very detailed and elaborate stage directions in the text of A Doll’s House in order to ensure the most realistic and lifelike staging of the play. The stage directions span several pages at the beginning of the play, describing in great detail the set, the furniture, the lighting, and the daily activities of the characters."}

